🧠 Multi-OS Home Cybersecurity Lab
Overview

This project documents the setup and configuration of a multi-operating system cybersecurity lab built on macOS using the UTM hypervisor. The goal was to strengthen my understanding of Linux administration, network segmentation, and remote access management by creating a controlled, simulated environment for experimentation and learning.

Objectives

Deploy multiple operating systems to simulate enterprise and industrial environments.

Establish network communication and SSH access across systems.

Troubleshoot virtualization and routing issues to improve system resilience.

Apply problem-solving discipline under realistic system challenges.

Lab Environment

Host System: macOS (iMac)
Hypervisor: UTM
Guest Operating Systems:

Ubuntu Server

Fedora

Debian

Kali Linux

Security Onion

Windows 10

Implementation Summary

Initial Setup & Configuration

Installed and configured all operating systems in UTM.

Resolved boot-order conflicts that prevented successful VM initialization.

Verified all VMs launched and performed as expected.

Network Configuration

Configured network interfaces for bridged networking, allowing inter-VM communication.

Verified connectivity using ICMP (ping) between machines.

Established local SSH access to Linux instances from the host.

Troubleshooting External SSH Access

Encountered SSH access restrictions from my classroom network.

Reconfigured router and reset modem to regain administrative control.

Began MFA recovery process to restore access to router management app.

Next goal: Enable IP routing and port forwarding for remote SSH access from external networks.

Key Learnings

Linux Fundamentals: Deepened command-line fluency and service configuration confidence.

Virtualization & Networking: Gained practical understanding of VM networking modes and IP management.

Security & Access Control: Encountered real-world authentication challenges involving MFA recovery.

Resilience & Adaptability: Each obstacle became an opportunity to refine troubleshooting discipline and system thinking.

Reflection

This project represented more than a technical lab—it was an exercise in control, adaptability, and understanding system leverage points.
Each configuration challenge required precision and strategy, reinforcing that effective cybersecurity depends not only on knowledge, but on the ability to remain composed under system adversity.

